Embedded React: View background gradients from JavaScript, dithered and damage-clipped.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, in the patch order listed there.
Changed files: engine/rendering/gradient.{c,h}, engine/include/er_scene.h,
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h, engine/scene/compositor.c,
engine/tests/rendering/{CMakeLists.txt,test_gradient.c},
bridges/quickjs/native_ui_bridge.c and
bridges/quickjs/js/src/embedded-react/index.d.ts. Proposed upstream as pull
request #283.

The engine could rasterize View gradients (ERUI_GRADIENT) but the QuickJS
bridge only exposed them for <Dial>. This adds a `backgroundGradient` style
({type: 'linear', angle | to, stops} or {type: 'radial', stops}) with CSS
linear-gradient()/radial-gradient(circle farthest-corner) semantics, including
`to <corner>` lines that follow the box's aspect ratio (new gradient_corner
prop, stored in existing padding after gradient_type). The rasterizer now
interpolates stops premultiplied (as CSS does), quantizes through a per-render
8.8 fixed-point ramp with a 4x4 ordered dither keyed to framebuffer coordinates
(no 8-bit steps on long dark ramps; flat colours stay exact), assembles only the
rows and columns inside the active clip, so a small damage rect over a
full-screen gradient costs only its own pixels, and reuses rows for
axis-aligned gradients (a horizontal ramp is assembled once per dither phase).

